// Inferpal extension registration
//
// $RootKey$       → VS private registry (privateregistry.bin, mounted on HKCU\...\{ver})
// $PackageFolder$ → extension install directory

// ── OOP host (commands, tool windows — new extensibility model) ──────────────────
[$RootKey$\VisualStudio\Extensibility\Extensions\Inferpal.bf3c1a2e-4d5f-4b8c-9e2a-1f7d3c6e8b4a]
"CodeBase"="$PackageFolder$\Inferpal.dll"
"FriendlyName"="Inferpal"
"Version"="1.0"
"Publisher"="EstaxNet"

// ── In-process MEF ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// There is NOTHING here, and that is the result of a measurement. A [$RootKey$\MEFComponent]
// key pointing at Inferpal.InProc.dll lived here for two days: it builds NOTHING. The MEF
// catalog of VS 18 is filled from the extension inventory (the Microsoft.VisualStudio.MefComponent
// asset of the manifest), not from the registry - no Microsoft component is registered that way,
// and VS rebuilds the _Config root at startup, which erases whatever is written there. What loads
// ghost text is the asset declared in source.extension.vsixmanifest, and nothing else.
//
// The key was removed once the hybrid switch had been measured loading (ActivityLog:
// Begin/End package load [Inferpal GhostText]). Why not leave it "just in case": the pkgdef merge
// is GLOBAL and stops at the first file it cannot process - measured on this machine, where the
// import aborted at the 2nd file out of 465 with E_ACCESSDENIED. Writing a useless default value
// into a root VS owns is risk taken on behalf of the other 464.

// ── In-process AsyncPackage (GhostTextPackage) ───────────────────────────────────
// Registers the traditional package that forces VS to load the in-process assembly
// at startup. Required so the MEF exports get instantiated
// (GhostTextViewListener.TextViewCreated, InlineDiffController, etc.).
//
// Inferpal.InProc.dll (net472), NOT Inferpal.dll (net8): devenv is a .NET Framework 4.7.2
// process and can load neither half of a .NET 8 assembly.
[$RootKey$\Packages\{6a7b2c3d-4e5f-4a8b-9c0d-1e2f3a4b5c6d}]
@="Inferpal GhostText"
"InprocServer32"="$WinDir$\SYSTEM32\MSCOREE.DLL"
"Class"="Inferpal.GhostText.GhostTextPackage"
"CodeBase"="$PackageFolder$\Inferpal.InProc.dll"
// The value name is "AllowsBackgroundLoad", NOT "AllowsBackgroundLoading". The latter is the
// name of the C# PROPERTY on PackageRegistrationAttribute; the registry value it writes is
// called "AllowsBackgroundLoad". This pkgdef is hand-written (GeneratePkgDefFile=false,
// CreatePkgDef.exe cannot read .NET 8), so nothing corrected the confusion. Measured: out of the
// 465 pkgdef files of VS 18, 59 write the correct name and the ONLY file in the whole tree
// writing the other one was ours. Cost: VS reads the package as NOT supporting background
// loading, so it IGNORES both autoload requests below (ActivityLog, AutoLoadManager: "is ignored
// because package does not support background loading", then SyncAutoLoadedExtensions
// "attempted the synchronous autoload feature") - GhostTextPackage is never loaded.
"AllowsBackgroundLoad"=dword:00000001

// ── AutoLoadPackages ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Loads GhostTextPackage automatically when VS has a solution open.
[$RootKey$\AutoLoadPackages\{adfc4e64-0397-11d1-9f4e-00a0c911004f}]
"{6a7b2c3d-4e5f-4a8b-9c0d-1e2f3a4b5c6d}"=dword:00000002

// Also loads GhostTextPackage when VS is open without a solution.
[$RootKey$\AutoLoadPackages\{f1536ef8-92ec-443c-9ed7-fdadf150da82}]
"{6a7b2c3d-4e5f-4a8b-9c0d-1e2f3a4b5c6d}"=dword:00000002
